IMPORT prelude

-- Medical Registration Act 1997 (Singapore) -- the six-year limit on complaints (ss 41, 42), the Disciplinary
-- Tribunal's grounds and orders (s 59D), when orders take effect (ss 59E(6), 59F) and appeals (s 59G).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Medical Registration Act 1997 -- discipline`

§§ `41, 42 -- the six-year limit`

-- s 41(1): the Council must not refer a complaint first made after the LATER of "(a) 6 years after the date of the act,
-- conduct or occurrence ...; or (b) 6 years after the earliest date on which the complainant had knowledge ..., or could
-- with reasonable diligence have discovered it". (4): "knowledge" includes what the person could have learnt from facts
-- observable or ascertainable, including with reasonable expert advice.
-- s 42: a late complaint must go to the President of the Disciplinary Commission, who assesses within 3 weeks whether it
-- is "in the public interest" to refer it anyway; if so, the Council must refer it.
--
-- A late complaint is not dead. It is diverted to a public-interest test.
DECLARE Complaint HAS
    `years since the conduct` IS A NUMBER
    `years since the complainant knew or could have discovered it` IS A NUMBER
    `the President considers referral in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the complaint` IS A Complaint
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the complaint is within the limitation period` IF
        `the complaint`'s `years since the conduct` AT MOST 6
    OR  `the complaint`'s `years since the complainant knew or could have discovered it` AT MOST 6

GIVEN `the complaint` IS A Complaint
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the complaint goes to the Complaints Panel` IF
        `the complaint is within the limitation period` `the complaint`
    OR  `the complaint`'s `the President considers referral in the public interest`

§§ `59D -- grounds and orders`

-- s 59D(1): the Tribunal may make orders where it finds the practitioner (a) convicted anywhere of an offence implying a
-- defect in character making them unfit; (b) guilty of improper conduct bringing disrepute; (c) guilty of professional
-- misconduct; or (d) "to have failed to provide professional services of the quality that is reasonable to expect of
-- him".
-- (2): (a) removal; (b) suspension "for a period not exceeding 3 years"; (c), (d) conditional registration or
-- conditions; (e) "a penalty not exceeding $100,000"; (f) written censure; (g) an undertaking; (h) any other order.
-- (3): a criminal conviction is "final and conclusive" in the proceedings and on appeal.
-- (4): if none of the grounds is found, the Tribunal "must dismiss".
--
-- Ground (d) is a standard of quality, not of misconduct. A doctor who falls below what is reasonable to expect is
-- liable to the same orders, up to removal, as one guilty of professional misconduct.
DECLARE Ground IS ONE OF
    `a conviction implying a defect of character`
    `conduct bringing the profession into disrepute`
    `professional misconduct`
    `services below the quality reasonable to expect`
    `none found`

GIVEN `the ground` IS A Ground
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Tribunal may make orders` IF
    CONSIDER `the ground`
    WHEN `none found` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the ground` IS A Ground
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Tribunal must dismiss` IF
    NOT `the Tribunal may make orders` `the ground`

DECLARE Order HAS
    `months of suspension` IS A NUMBER
    `penalty` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the order` IS AN Order
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order is within the Tribunal's limits` IF
        `the order`'s `months of suspension` AT MOST 36
    AND `the order`'s `penalty` AT MOST 100000

§§ `59E(6), 59F, 59G -- when orders bite, and appeals`

-- s 59F(1): an order for removal or suspension "does not take effect until the expiration of 30 days after the order is
-- made". (2): any OTHER order -- a penalty, censure, conditions -- "takes effect from the date the order is made".
-- s 59G(1): the practitioner OR the Medical Council may appeal to the High Court within 30 days after service. (4): an
-- appeal against an order under s 59D(2) or 59E stops it taking effect until confirmed, dismissed or withdrawn.
-- s 59E(6), 59G(5): unless the Tribunal orders immediate effect because it is necessary to protect the public, in the
-- public interest, or in the practitioner's best interests.
-- s 59G(2): heard by 3 Judges; "there is to be no appeal" from them. (3): findings on medical ethics or professional
-- standards are "final and conclusive" unless "unsafe, unreasonable or contrary to the evidence".
-- s 59F(3): on expiry of a suspension, rights revive "only if the person concerned has complied with all the terms of the
-- order".
DECLARE OrderKind IS ONE OF
    `removal`
    `suspension`
    `a penalty`
    `a censure`
    `conditions`

DECLARE Timing HAS
    `the kind` IS AN OrderKind
    `days since the order` IS A NUMBER
    `an appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Tribunal ordered immediate effect` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the timing` IS A Timing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order is in effect` IF
        `the timing`'s `the Tribunal ordered immediate effect`
    OR  (    NOT `the timing`'s `an appeal is pending`
         AND CONSIDER `the timing`'s `the kind`
             WHEN `removal` THEN `the timing`'s `days since the order` GREATER THAN 30
             WHEN `suspension` THEN `the timing`'s `days since the order` GREATER THAN 30
             OTHERWISE TRUE)

GIVEN `who` IS A STRING, `days since service` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal to the High Court is in time` IF
    `days since service` AT MOST 30

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is a further appeal from the three Judges` IF
    FALSE

GIVEN `complied with all terms of the suspension order` IS A BOOLEAN, `the suspension period has expired` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the practitioner's rights revive` IF
    `the suspension period has expired` AND `complied with all terms of the suspension order`
